          text: 'Episode 1: Why Is Everything a Rule?',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 4171),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 29219),
          text:
              'The first morning of the summer holidays should have felt completely free. There was no school bell, no uniform and no homework waiting. Mohsin and Mohsina had come over early to spend the day with Imaan and Akhlaq, and the four children were already planning football, a movie, cold drinks and, if the afternoon became hot enough, a water game in the garden.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 30465),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 70155),
          text:
              'Akhlaq walked into the kitchen expecting breakfast and stopped in front of the refrigerator. A large sheet of paper had been fixed to the door. At the top, Ammi had written: OUR HOLIDAY HOME PLAN. Underneath were a few simple rules. Screen time would come after basic responsibilities. Special snacks had to be asked for before taking them. Everyone would get a fair turn with shared things. The reserve water was not to be used for games. Nobody would leave the house without telling an adult. And between two and four in the afternoon, the house had to stay reasonably quiet because Dadi Jaan rested then.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 70815),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 99541),
          text:
              'Akhlaq read the list twice and turned towards his mother. “Ammi, is this a holiday plan or a list of things we are not allowed to do?” Mohsin came closer and immediately noticed the line about reserve water. “No water game?” he asked. Akhlaq pointed at the paper as though Mohsin had proved his case. “Exactly. We are only on the first morning. At this rate, by August we will need another refrigerator.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 100351),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 115339),
          text:
              'Ammi placed breakfast on the table and smiled. “You may complain after you eat.” Akhlaq sat down. “Is complaining also on a schedule?” Ammi replied that he seemed perfectly capable of doing that without one, and everyone laughed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 130027),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 132667),
          text:
              'The children played a board game, helped clear the breakfast table and then went outside with a football. By midday, however, the heat had become uncomfortable. Mohsin wiped his forehead and looked towards the garden hose. Akhlaq followed his eyes. No words were needed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 132667),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 156635),
          text:
              '“A water match,” Akhlaq said. Imaan immediately remembered the chart. “Ammi said no reserve water for games.” Akhlaq frowned. “The chart says reserve water. Maybe the garden hose uses normal water.” Mohsina smiled. “You are not asking a question. You are searching for an escape.” “I am seeking clarification.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 156635),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 175643),
          text:
              'They found Abbu in the sitting room. Akhlaq asked, in his most respectful voice, whether they could use the garden hose for ten minutes. Abbu asked what for. When Akhlaq explained, he shook his head. “Not today. The water supply has been irregular this week. Part of the rooftop tank is being kept as reserve.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 176003),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 196375),
          text:
              '“But there is so much water in the tank,” Akhlaq replied. Abbu looked at him. “Have you checked?” “No.” “Then how do you know?” “Because it is a large tank.” Abbu smiled slightly. “Large things can also become empty. For now, no water games.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 196931),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 224899),
          text:
              'The children walked back towards the garden. Mohsin whispered that parents had a very close relationship with the word no. Akhlaq agreed. Imaan reminded them that Abbu had explained the reason, but Akhlaq still looked dissatisfied. “We only wanted ten minutes.” Mohsina sat on the garden step. “We keep calling everything we want ‘only a little’. Maybe that is why adults keep answering.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 224899),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 250638),
          text:
              'Later that afternoon, another rule became inconvenient. The children wanted to watch an adventure film in the sitting room, but it was almost two o’clock. Ammi reminded them that Dadi Jaan would be resting. Mohsin promised to keep the volume low, but Dadi Jaan smiled from the doorway and said, “Your definition of low and mine are not always the same.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 250906),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 272772),
          text:
              'Abbu allowed them to watch in Akhlaq’s room as long as the volume remained reasonable. As they carried the laptop upstairs, Akhlaq muttered, “Another restriction.” This time Imaan stopped him. “You know why Dadi needs rest.” “I know.” “Then why are you talking as if Ammi made the rule just to stop us having fun?”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 273421),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 293691),
          text:
              'Akhlaq took a moment before answering. “Because sometimes it feels like every time we want something, somebody older decides whether we are allowed to have it.” Mohsin nodded. “Exactly.” Mohsina leaned against the wall. “Maybe adults forget that children also want to decide things.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 293933),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 322739),
          text:
              'Abbu had been passing the staircase and heard the last sentence. He stopped and said, “That is probably true sometimes.” “You agree?” Akhlaq asked. “I agree that children need chances to make decisions,” Abbu replied. “But does making decisions mean having no boundaries?” Akhlaq answered no. “Then perhaps the better question is not, ‘Why are there rules?’ but, ‘What is this rule protecting?’”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 323025),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 347707),
          text:
              'Abbu sat on the top step and explained that adults could be unclear, impatient or even mistaken. Being older did not make a person right about everything. Children could respectfully ask why a rule existed. But asking why to understand was different from asking why only because they hoped the answer would give them permission to do what they already wanted.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 349675),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 369675),
          text:
              'Akhlaq smiled. “So asking why is allowed.” “Yes.” “And if I do not like the answer?” “You may still dislike it,” Abbu said. “Disagreement does not automatically cancel a reasonable boundary.” Mohsina laughed softly. “That is the part he was hoping you would not say.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 369974),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 398131),
          text:
              'Abbu then asked them to imagine a hot pan on the stove. If a parent told a small child not to touch it, the danger would still be real even if the child did not yet understand heat properly. Sometimes a reason could be understood immediately. Sometimes it needed explanation. And sometimes a responsible adult knew something the child had not yet seen. “Like the water tank?” Imaan asked. “Exactly.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 398393),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 412739),
          text:
              'Abbu then asked, “Do you remember which Prophet comes after Hud, peace be upon him, in the stories you have been learning?” “Salih, peace be upon him,” Imaan answered. “And his people were Thamud,” Mohsin added.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 414848),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 433147),
          text:
              'Abbu nodded. He explained that Allah had given Thamud great abilities and blessings. The Qur’an describes how they settled in the land, built in its plains and carved homes into the mountains. Allah sent Salih (A.S.) from among them, calling them to worship Allah alone.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 433147),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 456515),
          text:
              '“Were they wrong because they were successful?” Mohsina asked. “No,” Abbu replied. “Just as strength was not the problem with ‘Ad, ability itself was not the problem with Thamud. A deeper problem appears in their story: what happens when people do not want to accept that freedom has limits before Allah.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 456911),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 485144),
          text:
              'Akhlaq looked back towards the refrigerator chart downstairs. “I knew the rules were going to enter the Prophet story somehow.” Abbu smiled. “Human family rules and Allah’s commands are not the same thing. Human rules can be mistaken and can be discussed. But Salih’s story can still teach us something about boundaries, rights and the danger of knowingly crossing a limit simply because we do not like being restricted.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 485776),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 513819),
          text:
              'Then he asked them a question. “If you can do something, does that automatically mean you have the right to do it?” Imaan said she could open Mohsina’s bag, but that did not make it her right. Mohsina said Akhlaq could eat all the dessert, but that did not make it fair. Akhlaq protested that he was always the food example. Mohsin said he could turn the television volume high while Dadi slept, but his enjoyment would interfere with her rest.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 515472),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 537987),
          text:
              'Abbu nodded. “Exactly. Ability and permission are not the same thing.” Akhlaq looked towards the garden hose. “And I can open the tap, but that does not mean I have the right to use the water however I want.” “Now you are reaching the point,” Abbu said. Akhlaq sighed. “I do not enjoy reaching points that reduce my options.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 537987),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 557684),
          text:
              'As they returned downstairs, Akhlaq stopped again in front of the refrigerator and read the line: DO NOT USE THE RESERVE WATER. This time he did not complain immediately. Instead, he turned towards Abbu. “I still want to know exactly how much water is there.” Abbu smiled. “Good.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 557684),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 583723),
          text:
              '“Good?” “You are asking to understand, not asking for permission to ignore the answer.” “So you will show us?” “Tomorrow morning.” Akhlaq folded his arms. “If there is loads of water, I would like the rule reviewed.” “And if there is not?” Abbu asked. Akhlaq hesitated. “Then perhaps I will review my complaint.” Mohsina smiled. “That sounds fair.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 584723),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 607491),
          text:
              'Abbu looked at the chart once more. “You will find something similar in the story of Salih (A.S.). There comes a point when a boundary becomes very clear. Then the question is no longer whether people understood it.” “What is the question?” Mohsin asked. Abbu answered quietly, “What did they do after they understood?”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 607491),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 619099),
          text:
              'For the first time, the holiday rules did not look to Akhlaq like a wall built around his freedom. Perhaps some of them were protecting things he had not noticed yet. Tomorrow, he intended to find out.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 619099),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 629227),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day: Can I Do It, or Do I Have the Right?',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 629227),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 634927),
          text:
              'Today, notice one moment when you can do something but should first ask whether you have the right to do it.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 634927),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 641707),
          text:
              'Before acting, ask: “Just because I can do this, does it mean I should?”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 643827),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 646027),
          text: 'Level-Up',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 646027),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 669175),
          text:
              'Choose one family rule you do not fully understand. Instead of complaining or secretly breaking it, respectfully ask: “Can you explain what this rule is protecting?” Then listen carefully before deciding what you think.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 669175),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 669176),
          text: 'Next Episode: But Tell Me Why',
        ),
      ],
